Specifically, the following cross-platform behaviours were derived from
Nushell's design (logic/approach ported to Aether, not verbatim source):

  - The Windows drive-letter prefix rule in lib/build/module.ae
    (`_has_windows_drive_prefix`) — the `Prefix::Disk` shape that
    nu-path relies on (a single ASCII letter + ':' optionally followed
    by a path separator).
  - The dual-separator rule in the native path helpers in
    lib/build/module.ae (`_path_is_sep`, `_basename`, `_dirname`,
    `_path_ext`, `_path_join`, `_path_to_slashes`) — on Windows both
    '/' and '\' are path separators (POSIX treats '\' as a filename
    char), the behaviour nu-path / Rust std encode.
  - The drive-prefix rule re-used in tools/aebcli/module.ae
    (`_has_drive_prefix`, a deliberate copy of lib/build's version) so
    the CLI's `path/to:name` target grammar does not mis-read a Windows
    drive path `C:\proj` as a `:name` synonym — same `Prefix::Disk`
    shape from nu-path.
  - The cross-platform process-termination mapping documented in
    docs/windows-cross-platform-notes.md (`taskkill /F /PID` on Windows
    vs `kill` on POSIX), derived from nu-system's `build_kill_command`.
  - The "no POSIX process groups on Windows — spawn normally" decision
    cited in the same doc, from nu-system's `ForegroundChild`.

